          %h2#exposition_of_a_general_abstract_formulation Exposition of a general abstract formulation
          %p
            We give a leisurely exposition of a general abstract formulation
            = succeed "-models," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            but trying to assume no other prerequisites.
          %p
            What is called an
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-dimensional
              = succeed "-model" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is first of all an instance of an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (to be explained). The distinctive feature of those quantum field theories that are
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                these arise from a simpler kind of field theory – called a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
                – by a process called
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %li
              %p
                moreover, this simpler kind of field theory encoded by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric data
                in a nice way: it describes physical
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
                equipped with some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} differential geometric
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
          %p We give expositions of these items step-by-step:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ExpositionQuantumFieldTheory"} Quantum field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ExpositionClassicalFieldTheory"} Classical field theory
